! parenthetical_entry.strap -- STRAP's PARENTHETICAL INTEGER ENTRY
! MODE, "(.n)A", from C28-6129 Section II printed pp.25-26 (PDF
! 29-30), heading "Field Entry Mode".  Read first-hand 2026-10-22,
! before a line of it was written (AGENTS.md rule 4).
!
!   "This mode permits any integer or pattern of bits to be stored in
!    any bit position of an instruction or pseudo operation that
!    produces binary output.  The general format for this entry mode
!    is:  (.n)A(n+1)
!    The symbol .n represents the BIT ADDRESS OF THE RIGHTMOST BIT of
!    the field into which the integer or bit pattern is to be
!    entered.  The integer A(n+1) is formed as an unsigned field, n+1
!    bits in length (because of the 7030 custom of addressing bits
!    starting with zero), and inserted into the leftmost n+1 bits of
!    the addressed instruction or data entry field by means of a
!    LOGICAL OR type operation.  Logical OR is used so that the
!    parenthetical entry may be combined with the existing contents
!    of the particular field addressed or with other parenthetical
!    entries."
!
! Every row below is one of the manual's own statements or a direct
! consequence of a sentence quoted in verify_parenthetical_entry.py.

DATA    SYN, (8)100.0

        SLC, (8)1000.0

! M1. THE MANUAL'S OWN INSTRUCTION EXAMPLE, printed p.25:
!   "In the following instruction:  E+I, (.8)41  the integer 41 is
!    converted to binary and OR'ed into the first nine bits of the
!    E+I instruction."
M1      E+I, (.8)41

! M2/M3. THE MANUAL'S OWN "same word either way", printed p.26:
!   "+(BU), DATA(.23)4, 20   or it may follow the offset
!    specification as in this illustration:  +(BU), DATA, 20(.23)4
!    In either use the result would be the same."
! Bits 0-23 already hold DATA, so these two rows are also the test
! that the combination is an OR and not a store: DATA is 001000 and
! the compiled field must be 001004, not 000004.
M2      +(BU), DATA(.23)4, 20
M3      +(BU), DATA, 20(.23)4

! M4. CONSECUTIVE ENTRIES OR TOGETHER -- the reason the manual gives
! for specifying OR at all.  Two entries into the same 24-bit field
! must both survive, and they must land in DIFFERENT bits or the
! check is vacuous: "(.23)4" is a one in bit 21 (4 = 100, rightmost
! bit at 23), so the second entry is written at bit 20.  The first
! draft used "(.21)1", which sets the bit "(.23)4" already set and
! would have passed with either entry dropped.
M4      +(BU), DATA, 20(.23)4(.20)1

! M5. A NEGATIVE PARENTHETICAL VALUE, printed p.25: "All
! parenthetical fields are regarded as unsigned by STRAP, so that a
! negative number is compiled as the complement, re 2**(n+1), of the
! magnitude of the number."  -1 in a 4-bit field (.3) is 1111.
M5      E+I, 0(.3)-1

! M6. MCP's OWN ROW, printed p.30 line 27, with the externals it
! names given here as integers so this deck stands alone.  The flag
! group it produces is the CHAIN FLAG, bit 25, and the listing
! prints it as "100".
M6      CW, (8)1037.0, 3, (8)1574.0(.25)1

        END, M1
